TI  - Central vein sign and paramagnetic rim lesion abbreviated counting methods for 
      diagnosis of multiple sclerosis: a systematic review and meta-analysis.
PG  - 106984
LID - S0009-9260(25)00189-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.crad.2025.106984 [doi]
AB  - AIM: The central vein sign (CVS) and paramagnetic rim lesion (PRL) are 
      neuroimaging biomarkers in multiple sclerosis (MS). Abbreviated CVS methods are 
      an alternative to percentage-based approaches. The pooled diagnostic performance 
      of Select-3 (≥3 CVS + lesions), Select-6 (≥6 CVS + lesions), and PRL (≥1 lesion) 
      is unknown. To summarise the diagnostic performance of CVS and PRL abbreviated 
      methods forMS. MATERIALS AND METHODS: We conducted a systematic review and 
      meta-analysis for studies that included two common abbreviated CVS methods 
      (Select-3, Select-6), and/or ≥1 PRL. The search was conducted in PubMed, Scopus, 
      and Web of Science. Studies were included if the sample consisted of participants 
      with MS and controls, and if the diagnostic performance of CVS and/or PRL was 
      extractable. Quality was determined according to the QUADAS-2 tool. Meta-analysis 
      was performed with a hierarchical model. Prespecified sensitivity analyses were 
      conducted. Publication bias was assessed with Deeks' plot. RESUTS: Twenty-nine 
      studies were included. Select-3 was available in 14 studies, Select-6 in 12 
      studies, and PRL in 17 studies. For Select-3, pooled sensitivity was 0.88 
      (0.79-0.94) and specificity was 0.77 (0.65-0.86), for Select-6 0.82 (0.70-0.89) 
      and 0.89 (0.82-0.94), and for ≥1 PRL 0.56 (0.42-0.69) and 0.99 (0.95-1.00). 
      Quality was impacted by risk of bias due to patient selection (10/15 CVS studies, 
      15/17 PRL studies). CONCLUSION: Select-6 and PRL are specific for MS, while 
      Select-3 is sensitive. To improve the quality of findings, future studies should 
      include strategies to avoid selection bias.
